window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"path":"book.html","title":"The Agentic Coding Field Manual — singular edition","text":"अ Man Learns Things · compiled 2026-08-26 · 16 chapters The Agentic Coding Field Manual One file. Everything offline. Master the modern agentic workflow — prompting patterns that survive production, UI craft, testing discipline, daily habits — with receipts behind every claim."},{"path":"builds/agent-cost-compass/dist/index.html","title":"Agent Cost Compass","text":"Know what a run costs before you ship the loop. Paste your agent's token pattern, see what it costs per run across LLM providers — plus a public changelog catching every quiet price change. Your agent's shape Tokens in / run Tokens out / run Runs per day Monthly figures use 30 days. Costs are list-price estimates at your exact in/out split — the blended $/M column shows the effective rate. Cached-input and off-peak discounts exist for some providers; see notes. Cost per run across models Sorted cheapest first · 19 models · prices as of 2026-08-25 · ①②③ mark the three cheapest paid options Tier All tiers Free ($0 walls) Cheap Mid Frontier Provider All providers Alibaba Anthropic DeepSeek Google Groq Mistral AI Moonshot AI OpenAI OpenRouter Z.ai Context window Any size < 32K 32K – 200K > 200K Unlisted Per-run and per-month cost per model at your token pattern, cheapest first Rank Model Ctx In $/M Out $/M Blended $/M Cost / run Cost / month Source · as-of Relative cost bar Gemini API free tier (AI Studio) — $0 within quota walls (ledger, 2026-08-18) gpt-oss-120b (free plan) — $0 within quota walls (ledger, 2026-08-25) Dots3-Note Preview (:free) — $0 within quota walls (ledger, 2026-08-25) DeepSeek V4-Flash — $0.0286 / run · $85.80 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) GPT-5.6 Luna — $0.032 / run · $96.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) GPT-5.4 Nano — $0.0325 / run · $97.50 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Gemini 3.7 Flash — $0.0563 / run · $168.75 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Qwen3.8 27B — $0.0625 / run · $187.50 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Mistral Large — $0.065 / run · $195.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) DeepSeek V4-Pro — $0.0858 / run · $257.40 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Kimi K2.6 — $0.135 / run · $405.00 / month (ledger, 2026-08-25) Kimi K2.7 Code — $0.135 / run · $405.00 / month (ledger, 2026-08-25) Claude Haiku 4.5 — $0.15 / run · $450.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) GLM-5.3 — $0.184 / run · $552.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Claude Sonnet 5 — $0.3 / run · $900.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) GPT-5.6 Terra — $0.32 / run · $960.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Kimi K3 — $0.45 / run · $1,350.00 / month (ledger, 2026-08-25) GPT-5.6 Sol — $0.6 / run · $1,800.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Claude Opus 5 — $0.75 / run · $2,250.00 / month (fetched-live, 2026-08-25) Free-tier walls (3) gpt-oss-120b (free plan) — Free-plan org-level caps: 30 req/min, 1,000 req/day, 8K tokens/min, 200K tokens/day. Cached tokens do not count toward TPM. HTTP 429 with retry-after on breach. Dots3-Note Preview (:free) — :free routes: 20 req/min always; 50 req/day until you have ever bought $10+ credits, then 1,000 req/day (openrouter.ai/docs/api-reference/limits). Multi-account evasion blocked. Gemini API free tier (AI Studio) — Free tier exists but no public fixed RPM/RPD grid remains - live per-project/per-model limits are visible only inside AI Studio. Tier 1 unlocks with $10 billing enabled. Published list prices from provider pages, retrieved 2026-08-25. Always confirm with the provider. Source link on every model name; provenance shown per row."},{"path":"builds/free-tier-atlas/dist/changes/index.html","title":"Changelog — Free-Tier Atlas","text":"Changelog Nothing recorded yet — and that's the honest state. The nightly diff pipeline lands in milestone M4; once it runs, every detected pricing-page change appears here as a factual diff with its retrieval date. back to home"},{"path":"builds/free-tier-atlas/dist/index.html","title":"Free-Tier Atlas — the IMDb of free tiers","text":"status: m1 scaffold Free-Tier Atlas The IMDb of free tiers — every dev service's $0 limits with cliff warnings (&ldquo;free until your app succeeds&rdquo;), plus a public changelog catching quiet pricing changes in the act. view changelog Charter No affiliate links. Ever. Refused in writing. No sponsored placements. Ever. Diffs stay factual forever. What changed on a vendor's pricing page is recorded as-is; opinions live elsewhere. Eat our own dog food: this site runs entirely on free tiers and publishes its own usage ledger. Stack (decided M1) astro · ssg-first, crawlable pages git-based yaml data → supabase at submission stage cloudflare pages + workers · dogfooding host github actions nightly diff · brevo alerts · posthog telemetry"},{"path":"builds/ncert-quiz-forge/dist/index.html","title":"NCERT Quiz Forge — chapter-wise practice, free forever","text":"class 9–10 · science · cbse & upsc-foundation Chapter-wise quizzes & flashcards for NCERT textbooks. Free forever, no coaching upsell, offline-capable for low-bandwidth India. browse chapters Chapters 0% No chapters yet The content pipeline is being built. Every question is drafted, then reviewed by a human against the actual textbook before it ships here. status: scaffold · drafting gate pending"},{"path":"builds/ncert-quiz-forge/dist/review/index.html","title":"Daily review — NCERT Quiz Forge","text":"spaced repetition · sm-2-lite · device-local Daily review Due cards from every chapter you have studied, queued here once per day. Progress lives in your browser only — export it anytime. Due queue 0 Nothing to review yet The scheduler arrives in a later milestone. Study a chapter quiz first and due cards will collect here. back to chapters"},{"path":"chapters/ch01-what-agentic-coding-is.html","title":"Chapter 01 · What agentic coding actually is — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"01 part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron Chapter 01 · What agentic coding actually is After this chapter you can explain what a coding agent really is — model, harness, loop — run your first session without getting burned, and name the moments when reaching for an agent is simply the wrong move. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 loop when to skip failure modes speed vibe vs agentic your build receipts exercises self-check beyond §1 Three parts, one machine Model predicts, harness acts, loop repeats — run the pass-by-pass simulator and watch a clear goal stop where a vague one wanders. ~6 min §2 When to skip the agent entirely Workflows vs agents, four honest \"say no\" cases, and why autonomy amplifies whatever it touches. ~4 min §3 Three classic failure modes Kitchen-sink sessions, trust-then-verify gaps, infinite wander — learn them by name and fix each in one line. ~4 min §4 An honest word about speed METR's randomized trial: predicted +24%, believed +20%, measured −19%. Your feeling of speed is not data. ~3 min §5 Vibe coding vs agentic engineering Never commit what you can't explain — how the vocabulary settled on \"agentic engineering.\" ~3 min §6 Where this lands in your build Chapter 01 compressed into one bounded build afternoon: the inner loop types, the outer loop keeps you honest. ~1 min §7 Receipts Every claim above traced to tiered sources — T1 anchors first, leads marked T3 and never cited alone. ~2 min §8 Try it yourself Sort real chores into workflow-vs-agent columns, amputate the verify step on paper, draft your first falsifiable stopping condition. ~15 min §9 Self-check Flip-to-reveal answers: model vs harness in one breath, the three failure modes, interrogating a 40% speed claim. ~5 min §10 Beyond this build Where the loop pattern ports outside coding, how harnesses differ in surface only, where agents break down. ~4 min The hype flattened the language. \"The AI built my app\" can mean four different things: a chatbot answered while a human typed every change, an autocomplete guessed lines, a scripted pipeline transformed text, or an agent genuinely acted — read the repo, ran the tests, edited files, retried. Those are not shades of the same thing. This chapter builds the mental model everything else in this book sits on. §1 Three parts, one machine Strip the marketing and every coding agent has three pieces. The model is the language model itself — a system that predicts likely text, one token at a time. It has no hands. It cannot open a file, run a command, or remember yesterday. Left alone, it produces text and stops. The harness is the program wrapped around the model. You never talk to a raw model; you talk to a harness that injects a system prompt you didn't write, hands the model tools — read this file, edit that function, run this command — enforces permission gates, manages the transcript so the model appears to remember the session, and feeds tool output back into the conversation. Practitioners describe a coding agent as exactly this: a harness wrapping a language model with invisible prompts and callable tools, where the ability to execute code is the defining capability. Two people using the identical model through different harnesses will have wildly different experiences — the harness is most of what you experience as \"the product.\" The loop is what makes it agentic. The working definition this book adopts: an agent is a language model running tools in a loop, toward a goal, bounded by a stopping condition. The model proposes an action, the harness executes it, the result feeds back in, and the wheel turns again — until the goal is met, the agent stalls, or the budget runs out. Remove the loop and you have a chatbot. A chatbot answers; an agent acts. scenario: clear goal — “make these three tests pass” vague goal — “make the login page better” run one pass reset scenario: clear goal · context 0% · passes 0 0% 25% 50% 75% 100% = context limit pick a scenario, then pace it one pass at a time · the meter scale is honest — 100% is the hard limit · run BOTH endings and compare them: that gap is the lesson Notice what is missing from that loop: magic. The agent is not thinking in any deep sense. Each pass, it looks at everything in its transcript and predicts a next action. The craft of this entire book is shaping what the loop can see, what it may touch, and how it knows it is finished. Here is the loop you own — wrapped around the machine's loop. Every technique in this book lives somewhere in this second chain. aim one goal, one success test → watch skim the plan before edits land → verify run the proof yourself → trust it? → commit accept, correct, restart clean one pass of the outer loop · human-gated · every step stays yours tip Write the stopping condition before you write the goal. \"Make the login page work\" is a wish; \"make these three tests pass without touching any other file\" is a stopping condition. Clear success criteria are the highest-leverage habit in agentic work — trial-and-error loops need a finish line they can actually detect, and vague goals are how loops wander. §2 What makes it agentic — and when to skip it entirely An agentic coding session differs from chatting with a bot in three concrete ways: the agent reads your actual files, runs real commands, and iterates on its own output without waiting for you between steps. Official documentation for leading harnesses is blunt that this is a different working relationship, not a faster chat window — you manage it more than converse. There is also a distinction practitioners lean on constantly. A workflow is ordinary code orchestrating a model through steps a human already understands: summarize this, classify that, format the result. An agent is the model directing its own process, choosing which tools to run and when. The canonical guidance from Anthropic's effective-agents essay: start with the simplest thing that could possibly work, and reach for an agent only when the task genuinely needs flexib"},{"path":"chapters/ch02-the-harness-landscape.html","title":"Chapter 2 · The harness landscape — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"02 part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral Chapter 2: The harness landscape Promise: after this chapter you can pick a coding harness deliberately — instead of copying whatever is trending — install opencode or Gemini CLI for exactly $0, and explain what skills, plugins, MCP and LSP do before you touch any of them. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · this landscape moves fast; the dates on every receipt matter first principles landscape opencode your build receipts exercises self-check beyond §1 What a harness decides for you Tool loop, permission gates, context handling — then set the three gates yourself in the permission simulator. ~8 min lab §2 The landscape, honestly compared Ten harnesses on license, the genuine $0 path and one standout each — every cell receipted, one archived row kept as a warning. ~4 min §3 Deep dive: opencode Surfaces, 75+ providers, plan/build agents, skills and plugins, LSP off by default, git-backed undo. ~5 min §4 Where this lands in your build Install one harness, then write its name and version into your Build 1 log so later advice maps onto real sessions. ~1 min §5 Receipts Comparison-table cells and the opencode deep dive, each traced to official docs, checked 2026-08-25. ~2 min §6 Try it yourself Install one $0 door, run one session, deliberately allow one action and deny another, make one config tweak. ~30 min §7 Self-check The three harness decisions, the two honest $0 doors, and why shipping LSP off by default is defensible. ~4 min §8 Beyond this build When a second harness earns its place, IDE vs terminal vs desktop trade-offs, where each tool breaks. ~4 min §1 First principles: what a harness decides for you A coding agent is two things wearing one trench coat: the model — the LLM doing the reasoning — and the harness : the program wrapped around it that turns a chat into somebody who can actually touch your project. Beginners assume the model is the product. Practitioners know the harness quietly decides most of the experience. Before naming a single tool, learn the three decisions every harness makes on your behalf. The tool loop The harness defines which tools the model may call — read files, edit files, run shell commands, search the web — how results feed back into the conversation, how many iterations are allowed, and when the agent must stop and report. Loop design is why two harnesses driving the identical model can feel like different products: one pauses to show you a diff after every edit, another barrels through twenty steps before surfacing anything. Your goal stated once, in words → Harness plans builds the prompt, picks tools → Model calls tools reads, edits, runs commands → Permission gate allow · ask · deny → You verify diff shown, loop repeats or stops the harness loop — owned by the tool around the model, not the model itself The permission model Every serious harness implements some version of allow / ask / deny : actions that always run, actions that prompt you first, and actions that never run. Where those lines sit is a values decision disguised as a settings screen. A harness whose default is \"skip the permission prompts\" is making a philosophical statement, not a technical necessity — and you inherit it unless you push back. Context handling The harness decides what enters the model's attention automatically: memory files, project instructions, skill descriptions, compiler warnings. It also decides what gets compressed away when a session grows long, and whether past sessions can be resumed or branched. This silently shapes output quality long before prompt-writing skill does — Chapter 5 goes deep on it. Four words you must be able to define You will meet these in every harness's feature list. Learn them tool-agnostically now: Skill — a written instruction file the harness loads on demand , teaching it a procedure (\"how we deploy\", \"how we name things\") only when relevant. Plugin — code you add that hooks the harness's own lifecycle events, enforcing rules or adding tools the authors never imagined. MCP — the Model Context Protocol, an open standard that lets a harness connect to external tool servers — databases, browsers, APIs — through one common socket. LSP — the Language Server Protocol: the machinery editors use for errors and \"go to definition\". A harness can tap those diagnostics so the agent sees the compiler's complaints directly. Beginners assume the model is the product. Practitioners know the harness quietly decides most of the experience. Watch the gate decide: a permission simulator No install needed for this one. Set the three gates below the way you believe a harness should behave, press run agent , and watch a three-step agent trace hit them one call at a time. You can even flip a gate mid-run — the next call obeys whatever the gates say at that moment . This is exactly what a harness's permission layer does for you on every single session, whether you ever look at it or not. permission gate simulator · interactive · runs locally, nothing leaves this page read_file(\"budget.csv\") pull a local file into the model's context ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny run_shell(\"python analyze.py\") execute a command on your machine ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny fetch_page(\"https://rates.example.org/current.json\") reach out to the network ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny ▶ run agent reset gates trace idle — set the gates above, then press run agent. §2 The landscape, honestly compared Nine harnesses were alive and documented at writing time, plus one instructive casualty. The table compares only what the sources below actually say — license, the genuine $0 path, and the one thing each does that others don't. Nothing here is sponsored opinion; every cell has a receipt. harness license $0 entry standout opencode (Anomaly) MIT Free OSS binary; local models or free-key clouds; reuses ChatGPT / Copilot / GitLab Duo subs; optional paid Zen/Go 75+ providers; TUI/desktop/IDE; skills + plugins + LSP; plan/build agents Claude Code (Anthropic) Proprietary No fre"},{"path":"chapters/ch03-models-without-money.html","title":"Chapter 3 · Models without money — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"03 part I · tools & access · L1 coral Chapter 3: Models without money Promise: after this chapter you can put a frontier-class model behind your agent today for literally $0, pick the right one of four free doors deliberately, and predict exactly where each door's published wall will interrupt you. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · every rate number below carries its own inline date — they expire monthly feel the walls four doors honesty break your build receipts exercises self-check beyond §1 Feel the walls: the free-door picker Five real doors, real published caps: slide a usage profile along the track and watch exactly where the 429 wall lands. ~5 min lab §2 The four doors, with their published teeth Subscriptions, free tiers, near-free APIs, local weights — every cap printed with its as-of date and its bite point. ~6 min §3 Cheaper is not identical Price ladders admit capability gaps; keep verification doubled until a cheap door proves out on your work. ~2 min §4 Where this lands in your build The picker above is Build 1's prototype — log door name, as-of stamp and harness version tonight. ~1 min §5 Receipts Provider pages behind every rate number, plus the training-data paper trail — T1 written policies first. ~3 min §6 Try it yourself Open one recommended door end to end, run a real session, fire a burst and log which wall bit you. ~20 min §7 Self-check Diagnose a mid-afternoon 429, defend the $10-credit rule as not-paying, name local's winning scenario. ~4 min §8 Beyond this build What changes when you eventually pay, org-level caps, and where local weights are simply unbeatable. ~4 min This is an on-ramp chapter, not an economics lesson — money appears here only where it blocks practice, and on day one it blocks almost everyone. Access is not one decision but four shapes , and every provider you will ever meet fits one of them: Ride an entitlement you hold. A paid AI subscription whose terms allow harness reuse powers an agent at zero marginal cost. Ride a published free tier. A vendor key with printed ceilings — requests per minute, requests per day. Pay almost nothing per token. Cheap open-weight APIs price agent habits in pocket change, especially off-peak. Run the weights yourself. Local models: no provider, no bill, no 429 — your hardware is the only wall. The transferable skill is not memorizing pricing pages; it is reading a published limit before you build on it, and knowing which shape you're standing in. Every harness from Chapter 02 reaches all four through the same three config fields — endpoint, credential, model id — so switching doors later costs an evening, not a semester. The transferable skill is not memorizing pricing pages; it is reading a published limit before you build on it, and knowing which shape you're standing in. §1 Feel the walls: the free-door picker Tables don't bruise you; walls do. Below are four real doors with their real published caps — nothing invented, every figure stamped with the date it was checked. Set a usage profile, then switch doors and watch the wall physically slide along the track. Push the daily slider past it and see exactly when your afternoon dies. One thing caps cannot show: free-tier data-training terms differ per provider and change without notice, so each door below prints its stated training-data policy inline wherever the source ledger holds one in writing — and every door without one is stamped plainly \"check current terms\". free-door picker · limit-feeler · published caps only · every door stamped with its as-of date · nothing leaves this page gemini cli · free personal google oauth openrouter :free never bought credits openrouter :free has ≥$10 credits groq free plan gpt-oss-120b local ollama your machine requests per day requests in your busiest minute 12 avg context tokens per request profiles: light practice · 25 one evening session · 120 all-day building · 400 team demo day · 1,500 no network wall — the limit is your hardware 0 500 1,000 1,500 2,000 req/day Three lessons the picker should have landed, independent of any vendor: neighboring doors differ twenty-fold (50 versus 1,000/day) for the identical request; per-minute walls bite independently of daily ones; and the local door deletes the wall entirely by moving the constraint onto hardware you own. Each door's date stamp also admits the quiet truth: these were the published numbers on their check dates . tip Pick ONE door tonight and take it end-to-end — practice beats planning. The four doors are interchangeable at the concept level (endpoint, credential, model id), so the skill built on any one transfers. The worst outcome is an afternoon comparing doors instead of shipping your first agent session. warning Every number in this chapter expires. Limits are repriced monthly, and doors die outright: GitHub Models — once a beloved free playground — was fully retired on 2026-07-30, redirects and all. Before betting a daily habit on any wall below, re-stamp it: open the linked page, compare the number, update your notes. §2 The four doors, with their published teeth door published caps (as printed) as-of where it bites Gemini CLI free tier 60 req/min · 1,000 req/day via personal Google OAuth 2026-08-25 Daily reset; requires the OAuth sign-in OpenRouter :free 20 req/min always; 50/day without ≥$10 lifetime credits, 1,000/day once crossed 2026-08 Credit threshold; multi-account evasion blocked Groq free plan (gpt-oss-120b) 30 RPM · 1,000 RPD · 8K TPM · 200K TPD 2026-08 Org-level; token walls; cached tokens exempt Gemini API (key) free tier No public fixed grid anymore — live limits shown per project/model in AI Studio only 2026-08-18 You must check your own console before building Mistral \"Free mode\" RPS + tokens/min + tokens/month — shown per-org in the console, not on a page 2026-08-12 Numbers exist only inside YOUR console Qwen Model Studio (new user) ~1M tokens per model, 90 days, Beijing/Singapore regions 2026-08-13 Quota expires; \"Free Quota Only\" mode hard-st"},{"path":"chapters/ch04-configuring-your-agent.html","title":"Chapter 04 · Configuring your agent properly — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"04 part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral Chapter 04 · Configuring your agent properly Promise: after this chapter you can write a memory file your agent re-reads every session, add skills that load only when needed, and bolt on guardrails the model cannot talk its way past — so the same mistake stops costing you every single morning. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · config surfaces move fast; receipts carry the dates why config instructions loader names you'll meet your build receipts exercises self-check beyond §1 Fixing it twice is a configuration problem Memory persuades, skills load on demand, guardrails compel — the three layers, and why config compounds. ~4 min §2 Watch instructions load Create and delete AGENTS.md files in a mock monorepo; watch nearest-wins strike through overridden rules live. ~4 min lab §3 The names you will meet AGENTS.md vs CLAUDE.md hierarchies, SKILL.md economics, hooks, and deny-beats-ask-beats-allow. ~6 min §4 Where this lands in your build Build 1's AGENTS.md opens at five seeded lines and grows only through the dated promotion rule. ~2 min §5 Receipts Standards pages and harness manuals, including Claude Code's load cap and the fail-open hook caveat. ~2 min §6 Try it yourself Write a five-placeholder AGENTS.md, verify it echoes back, then plant a contradiction and grade the result. ~20 min §7 Self-check Which layer blocks generated-file edits, who wins nearest-wins ties, five lines vs two hundred. ~4 min §8 Beyond this build Same anatomy under different names, monorepo nesting patterns, and diagnosing config cargo cult. ~4 min §1 Fixing the same thing twice is a configuration problem Here is the quiet tragedy of an unconfigured agent: every session starts blank. The loop has no memory of yesterday except what you leave on disk. So when the agent uses the wrong test command, edits a generated file, or writes commit messages in a style you hate, and you correct it in chat, that correction dies with the session. Tomorrow morning it makes the same mistake again, and you pay for it again, forever. Every serious harness offers three layers of standing configuration, and the distinction between them is the whole skill: Memory files — prose loaded into context automatically at session start. They persuade. The model reads them like advice from a colleague, and can ignore them like advice too. Skills / playbooks — instruction files kept out of context until a matching task appears, then loaded on demand. Economy: capability without rent. Guardrails — hooks, permission rules, plan modes. Code that runs before or after the model acts, regardless of what the model believes. Enforcement. The leverage is compounding. A fix typed into chat pays once; the same fix written into a memory file pays every future session, across every project that inherits it. Configuration is the only part of your agent setup that grows more valuable the longer you use it — which is why this chapter comes before the fancy patterns. A fix typed into chat pays once; the same fix written into a memory file pays every future session, across every project that inherits it. §2 Watch instructions load: the nested-files simulator Instruction files nest. There is usually a global one for your whole machine, one per repository root, and optionally deeper ones per package or subfolder. When the agent works on a file, most harnesses walk from that file's folder upward, loading every instruction file it passes — and when two files disagree about the same topic, the nearest file wins. Flip the toggles below to create or delete files, switch which file the agent is editing, and watch exactly what enters its context. instructions loader · interactive · nothing leaves this page editing packages/app/src/payment.ts editing packages/lib/src/format.ts the repo — tick a file to bring it into existence ~/.agents/AGENTS.md global user file · every project on this machine monorepo/AGENTS.md repo root · shared commands + conventions packages/app/AGENTS.md nearest to payment.ts packages/app/src/payment.ts packages/lib/AGENTS.md nearest to format.ts packages/lib/src/format.ts what the agent loads into context 0 active instruction lines tick packages/app/AGENTS.md while editing payment.ts — watch the repo-root test rule get struck through · nearest file wins ties, global fills the gaps, siblings never load Three lessons fall out of that panel. First, global defaults are the floor : personal preferences live far from any repo and apply everywhere. Second, nearest wins ties : the moment a package declares its own testing rule, the identical topic in the root file is dead letters for anyone working down there — which is why \"but my root file says so\" is not a debugging strategy. Third, siblings never leak : the lib package's purity rules never touch app code, because the loader walks up, not sideways. This nesting-and-precedence behavior is specified, not accidental, as the receipts below show. tip Start with five lines, grow on evidence. Do not generate a 300-line constitution on day one — long memory files dilute the short ones and cost context every session. Write five lines today. Then apply the promotion rule: the moment you correct the agent about the same thing twice, promote that correction into the file. Your memory file becomes a fossil record of your actual friction, not your imagined one. The same grow-on-evidence bar covers npm-installed plugins, which execute their code at startup before you see anything — treat every install like adding an npm dependency to your own app and vet it first; chapter 2 owns the full supply-chain warning. warning Config rot is real and actively harmful. A stale instruction doesn't just stop helping — it misleads: last year's \"always use library X\" turns every fresh session into a fight against your own file. Date every rule (\"adopted 2026-08, because Y\"), review the file quarterly, and delete anything you can't explain the reason for. An instruction file nobody rereads is"},{"path":"chapters/ch05-context-engineering.html","title":"Chapter 05 · Context engineering — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"05 part II · prompting craft · L2 olive Chapter 05 · Context engineering Promise: after this chapter you can keep an agent session sharp for hours — by managing what enters context, what gets compacted, and what carries across sessions. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · all token figures here are teaching approximations, proportionate to published measurements — your harness's real counts differ budget length costs what loads /compact memory subagents your build exercises inside this chapter 01 Context is a budget, not a bucket Attention is finite and degrades with volume — the craft is keeping signal density high, not fitting more in. ~2 min 02 The evidence that length costs accuracy Eighteen SOTA models degrade non-uniformly as input grows; a bigger window is storage, not immunity. ~2 min 03 What actually enters your context Standing rent vs working history, itemized — then toggle ingredients in the sandbox and watch sharpness sag. ~6 min lab 04 Compaction: what /compact actually does History collapses to a summary while standing rent re-presents every pass — you cannot compact away a fat MCP server. ~3 min 05 Memory that crosses sessions MEMORY.md and per-repo auto-memory persist conclusions — write conclusions, not transcripts. ~2 min 06 Subagents: the pressure valve, honestly sold A subagent reads 6,100 tok in its own window; the parent pays 420 — delegate breadth, keep judgment. ~3 min 07 Where this lands in your build The compass repo stays deliberately tiny: little standing rent, short histories, rare compaction. ~1 min Context is a budget, not a bucket Chapter 01 established that the agent re-reads its entire transcript on every pass of the loop. That transcript — the context window — is not a warehouse where stuff accumulates harmlessly. Treat it as a finite attention budget . Anthropic's engineering essay on context engineering puts it plainly: the goal is finding the smallest set of high-signal tokens that keeps the task solvable, because attention shows diminishing returns as volume grows. The craft is not \"fit more in.\" It is keeping signal density high enough that the right facts win the model's attention on pass forty, not just pass four. The same essay names three levers for long-horizon work: compaction (collapse history, keep conclusions), structured note-taking (persist facts outside the window), and sub-agent architectures (do heavy reading in a separate window, bring back a summary). This chapter is those three levers, plus the discipline that comes before all of them: deciding what you let in at all. The craft is not \"fit more in.\" It is keeping signal density high enough that the right facts win the model's attention on pass forty, not just pass four. The evidence that length costs accuracy Why budget at all? Because measured performance degrades with input length — even on trivial tasks. A 2025 study from Chroma Research tested eighteen state-of-the-art LLMs and found accuracy falls non-uniformly as context grows, with distracting material compounding the damage. Read the sourcing honestly: this is vendor research, marketing-adjacent by nature — but it was controlled, published with public replication code, and nobody has produced a credible reversal. The practical takeaway stands: a bigger window is storage, not immunity. A million-token context (remember Gemini's free tier from Chapter 03?) will hold everything and still attend to it worse. Degradation starts long before the wall. What actually enters your context Before you typed a single word today, your agent's window already held: a system prompt the harness authors wrote, your memory and instruction files, one-line descriptions of every installed skill, and — the sleeper — the API schemas of every connected MCP server, because the model needs to know which tools exist and how to call them. Official Claude Code documentation itemizes exactly this loading order. Then comes what the loop itself writes: your prompts, the agent's reasoning, every tool call, every file read, every command's full output. Two kinds of load result: Standing rent — charged every session, whether or not you use it: system prompt, memory, skill descriptions, MCP schemas. Working history — everything accumulated this session: pasted files, tool output, back-and-forth turns. Feel the difference with your hands. Toggle ingredients below and watch the meter; the sharpness readout applies the context-rot finding (fill level plus distractor load) to grade how much attention is left. When history has piled up, press run /compact and watch what survives. Every number is a rounded illustration, scaled to real published figures — the point is proportion, not precision. context budget sandbox · interactive · token values are teaching approximations, proportionate to published measurements standing rent — /compact cannot touch these system prompt harness-authored, loaded first, every session 2,400 tok memory file your instructions; re-read from disk after compaction 900 tok skill descriptions ×4 descriptions only — bodies load on use (measured: ~68 apiece) 280 tok MCP server · fetch-lite two tools, tiny schema 700 tok MCP server · browser schema measured head-to-head at ~3.6k 3,600 tok MCP server · github warned in docs: can exceed the context limit alone 12,400 tok working history — /compact collapses these pasted design-notes.md medium reference doc 1,800 tok pasted payments.js source file, 300 lines 2,600 tok pasted debug-log.txt distractor-heavy — rots attention fastest 7,500 tok session turns none yet — each “work” press adds ~3,200 tok of reasoning, tool calls and output 0 tok standing rent history /compact summary scale: 200,000-tok window (illustrative) ＋ work a few turns run /compact reset session post-/compact floor right now ≈ 3,580 tok — that is what compaction can never reclaim. toggle ingredients · pile on turns · watch sharpness sag · then compact and see what returns Run the experiment that matters: load everything including the gi"},{"path":"chapters/ch06-patterns-that-ship.html","title":"Chapter 06 · Patterns that ship — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"06 part II · prompting craft · L2 olive Chapter 06 · Patterns that ship facts checked as of 2026-08-26 shapes picker specs tests evals reflexion gauntlet plan-first your build exercises inside this chapter 01 Six shapes, one baseline The do-nothing baseline plus Anthropic's five workflow patterns — every added stage is a purchase that needs a reason. ~4 min 02 The pattern picker Assign four real chores a shape and read cost/latency/fragility scorecards — one assignment is deliberately wrong. ~5 min lab 03 Spec-driven development: rules before code Spec Kit's constitution → specify → plan → tasks pipeline; scale the spec's weight to the blast radius. ~3 min 04 Tests as the steering wheel Failing-test-first is the cheapest control surface; tests written after the code encode the model's own misreadings. ~3 min 05 Evals before iteration When no assertion can say \"good enough\": the eval ladder, and aligning an LLM judge on 25–50 human labels first. ~2 min 06 Reflexion, with an expiry date Written reflection between attempts improves later trials; the 80→91% digits are historical, the habit isn't. ~2 min 07 The gauntlet: the book's own engine Builder and critic split, fetched bars, blind binary verdicts — house craft, honestly labeled unvalidated. ~4 min 08 Plan-first mechanics Read-only plan mode, approval before edits, research delegated out — the cheapest pattern you own. ~2 min 09 Where this lands in your build quiz-forge's batch gates are evaluator-optimizer with a human judge, calibrated on a small sample first. ~2 min After this chapter you can name the five workflow patterns, pick the simplest one that fits your task — and run the book's own quality engine, a gauntlet loop, on work of your own. Chapter 01 gave you one loop: goal, passes, stopping condition, verify. This chapter is about shapes — the recurring structures practitioners wrap around that loop. Learn five names, learn when each earns its keep, and absorb the field's most profitable bias: start with the simplest thing that could possibly work. Most bad agentic work isn't caused by a too-weak pattern. It's caused by wearing a three-piece suit to dig a ditch. Most bad agentic work isn't caused by a too-weak pattern. It's caused by wearing a three-piece suit to dig a ditch. Six shapes, one baseline Before the five named patterns, the zeroth option: do nothing clever. One direct prompt, one session, a clear stopping condition. If that works — and it works far more often than beginners expect — you are done. Every pattern below is a purchase: more tokens, more latency, more parts that can disagree. Buy only with a reason. The five names come from Anthropic's building effective agents essay — the pattern-namers' own engineering blog, cited here as vendor material: Prompt chaining — decompose into fixed substeps, each feeding the next: gather → draft → check. Predictable and inspectable; worth it when the steps are genuinely distinct. Routing — classify the input first, send each class to a specialized handler. Buys focus; costs you a classifier to maintain. Parallelization — split one job into independent pieces (sectioning) or run candidates side by side and vote. Latency drops; disagreement becomes signal. Orchestrator-workers — a planner delegates to workers who cannot see each other. Strong on genuinely separable work; brittle when subtasks share hidden state. Evaluator-optimizer — one model generates, another judges against criteria, loop until it passes. The only pattern that manufactures judgment where no test exists. The pattern picker Four chores you will actually meet. Assign each a shape and read the scorecard: cost, latency, fragility, fit. The punchline repeats because reality repeats it — the simplest sufficient shape wins. One assignment below is deliberately, catastrophically wrong; go find it. pattern picker · interactive · scores are teaching heuristics, not benchmarks · runs locally, nothing leaves this page fix one failing test add a feature touching 3 files weekly dependency sweep subjective-quality landing page do nothing · direct prompt prompt chaining routing parallel fan-out orchestrator-workers evaluator-optimizer gauntlet simplest-sufficient picks found: 0 / 4 assign a pattern above — the scorecard lands here. tip Premature orchestration kills more sessions than weak models do. Before adding any stage to a workflow, name the specific failure it catches. If you can't name it, you're buying vocabulary, not reliability — ship the boring version. warning Verification debt compounds. Every unverified hand-off between stages multiplies into the next stage's inputs; by the time symptoms surface you can no longer attribute the failure. Verify at every seam — especially the seams you paid extra to create. Spec-driven development: rules before code When a task outgrows one sitting, the patterns need raw material: a spec. GitHub's Spec Kit turns that into a typed pipeline — /speckit.constitution records the project's non-negotiable principles, specify captures what and why while explicitly excluding technology choices, plan owns those technical decisions grounded in the constitution, and tasks breaks the work into ordered, checkable chunks. The launch essay's slogan is the right mental model: intent is the source of truth. The contributor deep-dive adds two corrections worth keeping: the constitution is what grounds the plan, and this is not waterfall — you re-enter any command when reality argues back. Right-size it. A constitution for a weekend script is ceremony; the simplest-sufficient rule applies to process shapes too. Scale the spec's weight to the blast radius of getting it wrong. Tests as the steering wheel The cheapest control surface for any pattern is a test the agent must satisfy. Willison's red-green guide reduces it to a one-line prompt: write a failing test first, confirm it fails, then make it pass. The confirmed failure is the point — it proves the test can fail, which is what separates a steerin"},{"path":"chapters/ch07-source-literacy.html","title":"Chapter 07 · Source literacy for agent builders — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"07 part II · prompting craft · L2 olive Chapter 07 · Source literacy for agent builders receipts dated individually · 4 of this chapter's 11 ledger rows were verified by direct fetch, the rest rest on documented chains - the fetched four are the METR pair, LiveCodeBench, and the Claude best-practices page · every claim traces to the book's source ledger coroner metr vendor studies gamed benchmarks devin arc memorization autopsy ledger machine your build exercises inside this chapter 01 Read a claim like a coroner Five wounds kill most AI claims — controls, measurer, funding, contamination, scope — plus the T1/T2/T3 tiers. ~4 min 02 The METR study, both acts Felt 20% faster, measured 19% slower — then an honest public walk-back worth more than never having blinked. ~4 min 03 Anatomy of a vendor study The Copilot \"55% faster\" figure fails every checklist row — same question as METR, opposite rigor. ~3 min 04 How benchmarks get gamed Leakage (SWE-Bench+), contamination (LiveCodeBench), governance (FrontierMath) — three documented failure modes. ~4 min 05 The Devin arc: death of a claim in public Launch video to walk-back in one month — and the T3-with-T1-corroboration pattern worth stealing. ~3 min 06 Memorization is not skill Claude models localize SWE-bench files 3–6× better — recall, not skill; a flagged preprint, honestly marked. ~2 min 07 The claim autopsy Commit verdicts on eight real evidence cards before any reveal; the ledger grades them all at once. ~6 min lab 08 The machine this book runs on The ~160-row source ledger behind every receipt — including the day it caught and dated its own error. ~3 min 09 Where this lands in your build Anything your agent asserts gets claim, source, tier, date — minutes to adopt, defensible afterward. ~2 min Promise: after this chapter you can stress-test any AI-productivity claim in about ten minutes — using the same three-tier system every receipt in this book was graded by. Hype arrives pre-packaged: a number, a logo, a chart going up and to the right. This chapter is the ten-minute routine for opening the package before you repeat what is inside — the book's own vetting discipline, turned around and handed to you. You will autopsy three real claims, dissect one famous vendor study, watch a benchmark get gamed three ways, follow one claim from launch video to public walk-back, and meet the machine that grades this book's own sources. Read a claim like a coroner Most AI claims die from one of five wounds, and you can check for all five faster than you can read the press release: No control group? Faster than what ? A credible number needs a comparison condition — same people, same tasks, randomly assigned with or without the tool. Without one, \"55% faster\" means \"faster than nothing, which we didn't measure.\" Who measured? Participants' feelings are not instruments; the gap between perceived and clocked speed is the best-documented effect in this field. Stopwatch, logs, or vibes? Funding disclosure? Whoever pays the piper picks the spreadsheet. Funding is not automatically disqualifying — undisclosed funding is. Contamination check? If a benchmark's problems sat on the public internet before a model's training cutoff, a high score may measure memory. Ask when test items were created versus when training stopped. Scope? A result holds in its studied population and nowhere else until proven otherwise. \"Experienced open-source maintainers on 2025 tools\" is not \"everyone.\" The professional skeptics Arvind Narayanan and Sayash Kapoor built standing checklists on exactly these questions at AI Snake Oil; the heuristics above deliberately rhyme with theirs. And when you chase sources, grade them before you trust them: T1 official docs, specs, repos, or established practitioners, verified before citing · T2 vendor blogs and aggregators, presumed marketing · T3 forums and social posts, leads only, never cited as fact without corroboration. The METR study, both acts You met this result in Chapter 01 as a warning about trusting your sense of speed. Here it is whole, because the second act matters more than the first. In July 2025 the independent nonprofit METR ran a randomized controlled trial: experienced open-source developers, real issues in repos they knew well, early-2025 agentic tools. Participants predicted a 24% speedup; afterwards they believed they had achieved about 20%. Measured reality: the AI group took roughly 19% longer . Data and code were public. Act two: in February 2026, METR's researchers publicly walked back confidence in their own method. Selection effects had crept in — skilled developers increasingly refused to be randomized into a no-AI arm — and follow-up work suggests late-2025 tools likely help more than the trial could show. Same institution, same public trail, updated conclusion. The lesson is not \"studies flip, ignore them.\" A source that publishes its own doubt, with dates and data attached, is worth more than one that has never blinked. Intellectual honesty is a behavior you can watch for. A source that publishes its own doubt, with dates and data attached, is worth more than one that has never blinked. Anatomy of a vendor study Now the counter-example this book keeps returning to. In December 2022, GitHub published research quantifying Copilot's impact: developers completed a task 55% faster . Run the checklist. Who measured? The participants themselves, timing their own work — the exact faculty METR later showed is unreliable. Control group? None. Funding? The seller studied its own product. Sample? Ninety-five people in a designed experiment. Each tell fires. Fair in both directions: this does not prove Copilot fails to help — it proves the 55% figure, as stated, was never established. The contrast with METR's design is the entire lesson: same question, opposite rigor. How benchmarks get gamed Benchmark scores look like measurements and behave like marketing. Three documented failure modes: Gaming by leakage. The academic "},{"path":"chapters/ch08-good-ui-with-agents.html","title":"Chapter 08 · Good UI with agents — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"08 part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 08 · Good UI with agents facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · every claim traces to the book's source ledger tokens contracts a11y floor screenshot loop your build visual bar exercises inside this chapter 01 Constraint layer one: design tokens Named values make off-palette choices unwritable — and the token format is a draft report, so verify importers. ~4 min 02 Constraint layer two: component contracts shadcn's AI-readable source and utility-class lattices make the compliant path the path of least resistance. ~3 min 03 Constraint layer three: the accessibility floor Contrast is arithmetic — 4.5:1 / 3:1 / 7:1 computed live in the lab, banned blue included to fail publicly. ~6 min lab 04 The screenshot loop, prescribed Screenshot → compare → fix against named acceptance criteria; chrome-devtools-mcp and playwright-mcp give it eyes. ~3 min 05 Where this lands in your build quiz-forge binds its UI to journey/design.md verbatim — tokens ship before the screens they style. ~3 min 06 The visual bar this book is judged against Ciechanowski essays as the blind-judged exemplar — and DESIGN.md itself built the way this chapter teaches. ~3 min Promise: after this chapter you can get non-generic, accessible UI out of an agent — by feeding it constraints (tokens, component contracts, a screenshot loop) instead of vibes. Ask three agents, on three days, for \"a clean modern landing page,\" and watch them converge: white canvas, placeholder-grey captions you can barely read, one deeply familiar shade of blue, soft blur shadows, generously rounded everything. The models aren't coordinating. With no local constraints, a language model regresses to the median of everything it has ever seen — and the median of the training data is precisely the generic interface you didn't want. The inverse observation is the good news. Simon Willison's first-person account from the March 2026 Pragmatic Summit records that inside a codebase with established conventions, agents follow the existing patterns \"almost to a tee.\" Hold the two facts together and the whole chapter falls out: the pattern-following machinery is the product. Give it patterns and imitation becomes leverage. Give it nothing and the median ships. So we supply patterns — three constraint layers any agent can consume, plus the accessibility floor that converts \"looks fine\" into a measurement. Give it patterns and imitation becomes leverage. Give it nothing and the median ships. Constraint layer one: design tokens A design token is a named value — a color, a radius, a spacing step — stored as data, separate from any stylesheet. For an agent it works as a machine-readable guardrail: the model doesn't have to interpret adjectives like \"warm,\" it resolves --paper to #F6F1E7 and stops improvising. Off-palette choices don't get argued against; they become unwritable, because every rule references a token instead of a literal. Honesty about maturity, per this book's protocol: the Design Tokens Community Group's format is published as a Community Group Draft Report — the de facto interchange standard for tokens, and explicitly, deliberately not a W3C Standard. Adopt it as the lingua franca between humans, agents and build tools, but verify your pipeline's importer before betting a release on it: { \"color\": { \"ink\": { \"$type\": \"color\", \"$value\": \"#201B14\" }, \"paper\": { \"$type\": \"color\", \"$value\": \"#F6F1E7\" } }, \"radius\": { \"card\": { \"$type\": \"dimension\", \"$value\": \"12px\" } } } Hand a file shaped like that to an agent before line one and \"make it fit the brand\" stops being a mood board and becomes a lookup. Constraint layer two: component contracts Tokens pin the raw materials; component contracts pin the assembled parts. The shadcn/ui docs state the quiet part out loud: open code you copy into your own repo, written to be predictable \"for your team and LLMs\" — an explicit AI-readiness claim, not a retroactive one. When a button, a dialog, a card already exist as readable source, the instruction \"use the existing dialog, don't invent one\" is enforceable: the agent can read the contract and compose instead of reinvent. Utility-CSS frameworks play the same game at a finer grain. Tailwind's utility classes plus theme variables act as a constraint lattice for generated CSS: every color the agent writes routes through something like bg-primary , resolved by the theme, so an off-palette hex has nowhere to land. None of this requires Tailwind specifically — the generalizable move is make the compliant path the path of least resistance . An agent greedy for the shortest output will take the lattice you built if inventing freehand costs more tokens than obeying. Constraint layer three: the accessibility floor WCAG 2.2's quick reference is the floor, not the ceiling. Two rows matter most day to day, both from Success Criterion 1.4.3 (Contrast, Minimum): text needs 4.5:1 , large text — at least 24px regular or roughly 18.66px bold — needs 3:1 ; the enhanced criterion 1.4.6 raises normal text to 7:1 . Contrast is arithmetic, which makes it the perfect acceptance test for agentic UI: no taste required, only math. The ratio comes from relative luminance: linearise each channel c ∈ [0,1]: c ≤ 0.03928 ? c / 12.92 : ((c + 0.055) / 1.055) ^ 2.4 L = 0.2126·R + 0.7152·G + 0.0722·B ratio = (L_lighter + 0.05) / (L_darker + 0.05) The contrast & constraint lab Every number below is computed live in your browser from exactly that formula — against this book's real palette, warts and all. Pick a text color, pick a surface, and read the verdict. One swatch is blue on purpose: it exists so you can watch the hue this book bans fail the test it deserves. Then flip the second switch and watch a token sheet rebuild a generic agent-produced card in place. contrast & constraint lab · interactive · ratios computed live from the WCAG formula · runs locally, nothing leaves this page 1 · text colour (foreground) 2 · surface (background) – : 1 contrast rat"},{"path":"chapters/ch09-testing-what-agents-build.html","title":"Chapter 09 · Testing what agents build — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"09 part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 09 · Testing what agents build facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · every claim below traces to the book's source ledger ordering the experiment browser evidence evals-lite practitioner moves in your build exercises Promise: after this chapter you can verify agent output like a skeptical QA lead — ordering tests so they can actually fail, demanding browser evidence for UI, and running review gates you can execute without reading a line of code. Agent output arrives pre-green. The diff looks clean, the demo works on the happy path, the summary says done . The trust-then-verify gap from Chapter 01 is widest exactly here, and this chapter is the closing move: the QA-lead habit of assuming nothing works until evidence says so. Three skills make up the habit — put the test in the right order , collect evidence instead of assurances, and build gates shallow enough that anyone can operate them. 1 Verification is an ordering problem Tests written after the code become biographies, not specs — the tautology trap and why a confirmed red is the whole point. ~4 min 2 The experiment: same feature, two orders Same divide page, same random defect, two workflows: code-first ships it green, test-first catches it pre-ship. ~5 min lab 3 Browser evidence for UI: the trace as a review gate Playwright traces a non-coder can operate — CLI bursts vs MCP sessions, cheap evidence vs shared page state. ~5 min 4 Evals-lite: grading what assertions can't Task + graders + outcome: grade real outcomes, read transcripts yourself — five graded examples beat vibes. ~3 min 5 Two practitioner moves worth stealing The conformance-suite trick and the evidence mandate: \"show me the command and its output.\" ~3 min 6 Where this lands in your build Compass verify 9/9 and quiz-forge batch gates already run this chapter; Chapter 12 wires them into CI. ~3 min &sect;1 Verification is an ordering problem A test is an instruction to the loop. Chapter 06 introduced tests as the steering wheel; what it could only name in passing, this chapter makes you feel : the wheel only steers if it was bolted on before the car moved. Write the test first and it is a specification — the code must come to it . Write the test after and it is a biography — it can only describe what the code already does, misreadings included. That second path has a name: the tautology trap . Field synthesis of agentic development datasets found that tests written after the code tend to encode the model's own misreadings, producing suites that pass while the behavior is wrong — the test agrees with the code because it learned correctness from the code. Practitioner defenses exist (review contract tests as specification, audit for hermeticity), but the cheapest defense is structural and costs one sentence of prompt. Willison's red-green guide reduces it to exactly that: write a failing test first, confirm it fails, then make it pass. The confirmed failure is the entire point — it is proof the test can fail, which is what separates an instrument from a decoration. The same skepticism applies to the agent's own reports. An agent that ends with \"the feature works\" has asserted; an agent that boots the server, hits the endpoint, and pastes the transcript has exercised. Practitioner rule from the field: agents must exercise what they build and show the evidence, not declare success — and the corollary for you is that \"it passed\" is a claim, while a captured command and its output are a fact. \"It passed\" is a claim, while a captured command and its output are a fact. &sect;2 The experiment: same feature, two orders Judge the ordering argument yourself. Below, two teams build the identical tiny feature: a page that divides two inputs and shows the result. Unknown to both, one of three defects rides along — a division-by-zero leaking raw Infinity , a negative input quietly losing its sign, or floating-point garbage in the decimals. Which defect is drawn is randomized each round, shuffled so repeated plays cover all three. Track A codes first and writes tests afterward. Track B writes the failing test first. Press both through, watch the same bug meet two different workflows, and let the scoreboard keep the honest accounting: bugs caught per minute of verification, across as many rounds as you play. catch-the-bug lab · one feature, two orders · randomized defect each round · runs locally, nothing leaves this page ▶ start round 1 reset scoreboard round 0 · defect undrawn · press start TRACK A code first · tests written after idle — waiting for round start. press: next A step TRACK B failing test first · code second idle — waiting for round start. press: next B step scoreboard · bugs caught per minute of verification no rounds played yet. A spends less time verifying; B spends more. Watch what each minute buys. Play at least three rounds so the shuffle deals you all three defects. Then read the totals the way a QA lead would. Track A posts the fastest verification time in the building and a permanently perfect green suite — and a bug-catching rate of zero, forever, because a test that merely restates the code cannot fail. Its green light certifies agreement, not correctness: 1 &divide; 0 shipping as \"Infinity — verified ✓\" is the tautology trap made visible. Track B looks slower on paper and catches every defect at step one, because the red run happens before the code exists and names the violation in the spec's own words. That asymmetry is the whole chapter: verification time buys nothing without sensitivity, and sensitivity comes from order. Verification time buys nothing without sensitivity, and sensitivity comes from order. tip Make the agent write the check before the fix. One line of prompt does it: \"write a failing test first, confirm it fails, then make it pass.\" The confirmation step is non-negotiable — an unconfirmed red is a guess, a confirmed red is a measurement. warning Green ≠ correct. A test that cannot fail is decora"},{"path":"chapters/ch10-debugging-when-it-breaks.html","title":"Chapter 10 · Debugging when it breaks — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"10 part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 10 · Debugging when it breaks facts checked as of 2026-08-26 reproduce escape hatches bisect wreckage evidence in your build exercises Promise: after this chapter you can drive a disciplined debug — reproduce, hypothesize, bisect — instead of watching an agent thrash, and you know exactly when to pull the plug. Breakage is not the exception in agentic work; it is Tuesday. The difference between a beginner and a practitioner is not that practitioners break less — it is that their next move is scripted. Beginner: paste the failure into the agent, receive a confident edit, repeat until either the bug dies or the diff becomes landfill. Practitioner: capture the symptom, force it into a proof, cut the search space in half, and reach for the escape hatches early. This chapter drills that script until it is muscle memory. 1 Reproduce before you explain Repro recipe first, failing test second — fix the root cause; suppression just moves the bomb. ~4 min 2 Escape hatches, straight from the docs /rewind, clear after two missed corrections, hook-enforced caps — plus one-log, fast-loop ground rules. ~4 min 3 Bisect: halving beats walking Play the sixteen-commit race: midpoint tests finish in four moves, linear walks drag past eight. ~5 min lab 4 Reading the wreckage Trace timelines with Errors/Console/Network tabs, stack traces read backward, logs greppable in one file. ~3 min 5 Evidence, not assurances Pace a real thrash loop yourself, then pull the cheapest hatch early — repro first, drama last. ~5 min lab 6 Where this lands in your build A pinned-case verifier committed beside the feature: breakage gets replayed, not remembered. ~2 min &sect;1 Reproduce before you explain A bug you cannot summon on demand is a bug you cannot prove dead. Before any theory, before any edit, get a repro recipe : the exact input, the exact click or command, the expected result versus the actual one, written down. Official best-practices documentation for leading harnesses encodes this as the first discipline of debugging with an agent — reproduce the issue, and fix the root cause , not the symptom. Those are two rules, not one. Suppressing an error (wrapping the crash site in a silent catch, returning a default value so the page renders) makes the pain stop while the disease spreads. The upgrade path from repro is a failing test : encode the recipe as an assertion that goes red today and must go green tomorrow. This does three jobs at once. It converts a vague complaint into a pass/fail switch. It stops the agent — or you — from declaring victory prematurely, because the proof is executable. And it hands every later tool, including bisection below, the automatic good/bad oracle they need. Agents will not do this unprompted; demanding the failing test is your move. A bug you cannot summon on demand is a bug you cannot prove dead. tip Paste the exact error text, never a paraphrase. Not \"there's a type error somewhere in checkout\" — the full message, the file, the line number, the stack. Models route on specifics; a paraphrase sends them hunting in the wrong county. Same rule for your own notes: the exact string is searchable, greppable, and comparable after the next attempt. &sect;2 Escape hatches, straight from the docs Runaway correction loops are so predictable that harness manuals document the exits officially. Know them by name: Rewind to a checkpoint. Sessions checkpoint as they go; /rewind in Claude Code restores conversation and code to an earlier point, so a bad direction costs minutes, not an evening. Clear after two failed corrections. Official guidance draws a bright line: when corrections keep missing, stop correcting. Start a fresh session with clean context and lead with the exact error text. Hook-enforced iteration caps. Hooks are deterministic guardrails you write yourself (the ch04 pattern), so a custom Stop hook can count correction rounds and cut a spinning loop off at a fixed cap — but no harness ships an anti-thrash mechanism by default: stock Stop-hook behavior is verification gating, checking the work passes before the turn ends. Caps are a pattern you install, not a stock mechanism. Beneath the hatches sits environment quality — Ronacher's advice from a year of agentic coding, and it holds: keep everything logged to one file you can actually read afterwards; keep the feedback loop fast enough to rerun after every change; prefer commands that fail loudly; and when context compaction hits a long session, abort and restart rather than nursing a degraded one. A slow loop and a murky log are how two-minute bugs become two-hour sessions. A slow loop and a murky log are how two-minute bugs become two-hour sessions. &sect;3 Bisect: halving beats walking Oldest trick in the book, and it survives agents perfectly. When you have an ordered history — commits, config edits, deployed changes — and something flipped from working to broken somewhere along it, do not walk the list. Test the middle item. Working? The culprit is in the back half. Broken? The front half. Either way you just deleted half the suspects with one test. Sixteen candidates fall to four tests, because ⌈log₂ 16⌉ = 4; a linear walk drags through up to fifteen tests and averages more than eight. Git productized the loop decades ago: git bisect checks out midpoints, takes your good/bad verdicts, and names the first bad commit — and git bisect run hands your test script to the loop so git plays both sides and you never click anything. Feel the race before you memorize the math. Sixteen commits below, one of them introduces a bug — re-randomized every reset. Pick a strategy, then click commits to test them. Par is 4. bisect race · interactive · runs locally, nothing leaves this page · bug re-randomized every reset strategy: binary bisection strategy: linear scan reset · reshuffle bug moves 0 · par 4 ✓ passes · ✗ shows the bug · dashed = ruled out, outside the suspect range If you played bisec"},{"path":"chapters/ch11-brownfield-reality.html","title":"Chapter 11 · Brownfield reality — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"11 part III · building real things · L3 jade Chapter 11 · Brownfield reality facts checked as of 2026-08-26 facts checked as of 2026-08-26 brownfield understand first repo maps vocabulary legibility specs in your builds exercises Promise: after this chapter you can point an agent at a big, unfamiliar codebase and get safe, surgical changes instead of collateral damage. 1 The repo you didn't write Branch before you brief: the zero-cost baseline every other brownfield safety move leans on. ~4 min 2 Understand before you edit Explore → plan → implement → commit with a verify-first precondition; infinite exploration timeboxed. ~5 min 3 Maps of big repos: ranked sketches Aider's graph-ranked map under a token-budget slider — watch what the agent knows vs misses per change. ~5 min lab 4 A vocabulary for touching nothing Fowler's behavior-preserving moves decompose risky edits into steps an agent can execute mechanically. ~2 min 5 Make the codebase legible to machines Local reasoning and grep-ability over cleverness; find the exemplar module and point the agent at it. ~3 min 6 Specs on inherited ground Spec Kit's existing-projects track; EPAM's compounding case read with the vendor badge in view. ~4 min 7 Where this lands in your builds Compass stayed small by design; quiz-forge maps NCERT's structure instead of \"fixing\" it. ~2 min &sect;1 The repo you didn't write Every technique so far in Part III assumed canvas you controlled. Real careers are spent somewhere else: inside codebases that existed before you arrived, with years of decisions folded into filenames, half-documented conventions, and couplings nobody remembers making. Practitioners call this brownfield — built-on land, as opposed to empty greenfield — and agents change its difficulty unevenly. An agent reads faster than you ever will and does not tire. But it also arrives with zero inherited intuition: no memory of the incident that made the retry logic so paranoid, no feel for which of the four similarly-named modules is the live one. The hard constraint underneath everything in this chapter is arithmetic. A frontier working context window holds roughly 200,000 tokens at best — the budget Chapter 05 metered; the raw source of even a 400-file repository typically exceeds what fits unfiltered. Nobody — not you, not the model — can hold the whole system in mind, and that gap is exactly why maps exist. Greenfield work hides this because the codebase grows alongside your understanding of it; brownfield puts you and the agent at the bottom of an existing mountain on day one. The craft is compression with judgment: build a small, ranked sketch of the territory, expand it deliberately where the change will land, and only then edit. One move outranks everything else on inherited ground, and it costs nothing: branch before you brief. Create a working branch — or at minimum a commit of the current state — before any agent touches the code. Every other safety move in this chapter is reversible only because of that baseline: a bad agent run becomes a throwaway branch you delete, not a wound you excavate from your working tree. Fold pinning into the same beat: before any editing, have the agent write characterization tests around the code you're about to change, so the suite — not your memory — judges whether behavior moved. Branch before you brief: every other safety move in this chapter is reversible only because of that baseline. &sect;2 Understand before you edit The tool-agnostic discipline has four phases, and the documentation leads with a precondition rather than a step: give the agent a way to verify its work — a test command, a build, a script whose exit code doesn't lie — before asking for any change. Then: Explore: read-only investigation — find the files involved, follow the references, locate the tests pinning current behavior. Plan: write the intended change as a diff-shaped proposal — which files, which functions, what stays untouched — and check it against what exploration found. Implement: only now touch anything, smallest change first, checked against that verification harness immediately. Commit: a reviewed diff lands as a commit on your baseline branch — the run ends saved, not left dangling in a working tree. Official best-practices documentation for agentic harnesses encodes exactly this explore-plan-implement-commit arc for unfamiliar codebases, leading with the verify-first rule and including delegating investigation to subagents so the main session's context stays clean for the change itself. The documented anti-pattern is just as instructive: infinite exploration — the session where the agent keeps reading, more files, more grep hits, another layer of indirection, and never commits to a plan, because \"understand everything\" was silently treated as the goal. You saw this loop's cousin in Chapter 01: a goal with no detectable finish line wanders. In brownfield work the wander wears a respectable disguise (it looks like diligence), so timebox it: exploration ends when you can state the plan, not when curiosity runs out. explore read-only · find files, tests, couplings → plan diff-shaped proposal · what stays untouched → implement smallest change · verified at once → commit reviewed diff · lands on your branch diff review understand-before-edit · exploration ends when the plan can be written, not when curiosity runs out &sect;3 Maps of big repos: ranked sketches If the whole repo cannot fit in context, what does? Every serious answer is some version of a repo map : a compressed outline of many files at once, spending its small token budget where visibility pays most. The reference implementation is aider's, worth knowing mechanically because the idea transfers to any harness. Aider parses every source file with tree-sitter to extract symbol definitions and references, builds a graph of which symbols refer to which, and ranks files by importance — files referenced from many places, and the entry points that anchor reference"},{"path":"chapters/ch12-pipelines-that-prevent-bloat.html","title":"Chapter 12 · Pipelines that prevent bloat — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"12 part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum Chapter 12 · Pipelines that prevent bloat facts checked as of 2026-08-26 gates reviewers bots calendar in your builds exercises Promise: after this chapter you can wrap your agent workflow in gates that keep a project healthy for months — bloat becomes something the pipeline catches, not something you notice too late. Every project rots by default, and \"bloat\" is bigger than file size. Builds break. Dependencies fall behind and collect vulnerabilities. Links die. The README describes a program that no longer exists. The spec promises a page the code never shipped. None of this arrives as an alarm; it arrives as a slow tax on every future session — and coding agents quietly raise the rate. An agent never feels embarrassed by the stale README, so it trusts it; the next session inherits yesterday's drift as today's facts. You met this in Chapter 01 as stale breadcrumbs and bloated memory files. Willpower will not fix it — vigilance fails by week three. Plumbing will. 1 A gate is a check that runs so you don't have to remember Automatic, binary, fast: five gate families staged cheapest-first, plus a paste-sized build workflow. ~5 min 2 Reviewers that never tire — but must be bounded @claude and Codex cloud review PRs under turn caps and least privilege; accountability stays human. ~4 min 3 Bots that file the chores Dependabot and Renovate file upgrade PRs on schedule — one bot per ecosystem, workflows included. ~3 min 4 The pipeline on a calendar Six simulated weeks with gates on/off — same incidents both runs, wildly different debt boards. ~5 min lab 5 Where this lands in your builds Both companion builds already live behind §9: PR-per-milestone, conventional prefixes, reviewed diffs. ~2 min &sect;1 A gate is a check that runs so you don't have to remember Tool-agnostically, a delivery pipeline is one idea: every change enters the project through the same doorway — a branch turned into a pull request — and the doorway is rigged with gates : automated checks that run on arrival. A good gate has three properties. It is automatic (no willpower involved), binary (exit code, not vibes — the verify ritual from Chapter 01, industrialized), and fast (feedback while the change is still fresh in anyone's head). Five families of gate cover nearly everything that rots, staged by rising cost of adoption: Correctness — does it build, do tests pass. Catches the most common agent failure: plausible code nobody ever ran. Hygiene — link checkers, formatters. Catches decay nobody commits but everybody creates. Currency — dependency bots filing upgrade chores as tickets-with-diffs attached. Drift — scheduled jobs that compare documentation and specs against reality, on a calendar instead of at merge time. Review — a human, or a bounded agent, reading the diff before it merges. Last: the expensive, nuanced gate. branch never brief an agent on main → pull request small diffs · conventional commits → gates run build · links · review · bumps → merge only when all green all green? weekly sweep docs-sync · triage · upgrades the doorway · PR-time gates catch what a change breaks · calendar gates catch what time breaks tip Add the cheapest gate first: the build must pass . It is a few lines of CI config, costs almost nothing to maintain, and intercepts the single most frequent way agents ship damage — code that looks done and was never executed. Stage the rest by rising cost: link check, dependency bot, scheduled sync, and agent review last, because it is the expensive, nuanced one. A pipeline you grow one cheap gate at a time survives; one you try to install wholesale usually doesn't. The thesis gate is paste-sized too. Save this generic template as .github/workflows/build.yml and every pull request runs the build — swap the two run: steps for whatever \"it builds\" means in your stack: # .github/workflows/build.yml · generic-template · cheapest gate first name: build on: pull_request jobs: build: runs-on: ubuntu-latest permissions: contents: read steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v7 - uses: actions/setup-node@v7 with: node-version: 24 - run: npm ci - run: npm run build &sect;2 Reviewers that never tire — but must be bounded The review gate no longer has to be you-alone. Anthropic ships an official Claude Code GitHub Action: mention @claude in a pull request and a bounded agent investigates the diff, asks questions, and can push fixes; an automation mode lets it act on triggers such as new issues, and a --max-turns setting caps how many agentic turns each invocation gets — which is exactly what it sounds like, a spending limit on autonomy. Authentication rides in GitHub secrets; the action is MIT-licensed, works with Bedrock/Vertex/Foundry backends, and its repo carries a security guide whose three headings are worth memorizing: access control (who can summon the agent), least-privilege permissions (what it may touch), and verified commits (signed, so its output is attributable). OpenAI's answer lives in the cloud: Codex cloud tasks — delegable with @codex , runnable in parallel, with first-class PR-review flows — driven by the same agent as the Apache-2.0 Codex CLI, signed in with a ChatGPT plan or an API key. The honest caveat comes from Chapter 01 and never expires: accountability stays human. An agent reviewer is a tireless first pass that catches the mechanical sins — scope creep, missed call sites, untested paths — while you keep the judgment calls. Bound its turns, restrict its permissions, read its findings, and it compounds your attention instead of replacing it. &sect;3 Bots that file the chores Currency gates are solved problems; adopt them rather than hand-roll them. GitHub's Dependabot reads a dependabot.yml and files upgrade PRs on a schedule — and it covers more than libraries: the github-actions ecosystem updates the pinned workflows themselves, closing the loop where your pipeline's own skeleton ages: # .github/dependabot.yml — two ecosystems, one chore queu"},{"path":"chapters/ch13-shipping-for-$0.html","title":"Chapter 13 · Shipping for $0 — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"13 part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum Chapter 13 · Shipping for $0 facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · every hosting number below carries its own check date — they expire quietly ritual wall finder four hosts preview deploys legal wall deploy release notes your build receipts exercises self-check beyond Promise: after this chapter you can put your project on a real public URL today — today, not someday — at literally zero cost, and name the exact wall your traffic will hit first on whichever host you pick. s1 The whole ritual, before any vendor Static folder → git repo → one-time host connect; afterwards shipping costs one command forever. ~4 min s2 Feel the walls: the wall finder Dial visitors and builds against four hosts' published caps and watch which wall trips first. ~6 min lab · wall finder s3 The four hosts, verbatim, dated The caps printed plainly with their check dates, so you can audit the finder against them. ~3 min s4 Preview deploys: review the real thing, not a screenshot Every branch gets its own URL before merge — click the actual change on an actual phone. ~3 min s5 One host's wall is made of law GitHub's terms bar commercial hosting on Pages — a wall no dial measures, discovered by email. ~4 min s6 Deploy = one command, after setup Setup is an afternoon; deployment collapses to git push — no custom scripts before push ten. ~3 min s7 Let the agent write the release notes Hand over merged commits and house style; the agent drafts, you verify against the diff, then it ships. ~3 min s8 Where this lands in your build How the compass picked Pages and the quiz forge picked Cloudflare — and the dated line your BUILD.md owes. ~3 min s9 Receipts Tiered, dated sources behind every cap in the chapter, cross-chapter rows flagged. ~2 min s10 Try it yourself — ship something today Hello-public-world on a real URL tonight, break the finder on purpose, stamp your BUILD.md. ~30 min s11 Self-check Cloudflare vs Netlify at 2,000 pushes; Pages' invisible wall; when paid hosting earns itself. ~3 min s12 Beyond this build Custom domains, analytics-lite, and the moment static hosting stops being enough. ~4 min The whole ritual, before any vendor Shipping sounds like infrastructure. It is three boring steps, and every free host on earth is just a different brand of the same three: Produce a static output. A folder of HTML, CSS, and JS that any web server could serve as-is. Plain hand-written pages qualify; so does whatever build step your generator runs. If double-clicking index.html shows your site, step one is done. Put that folder in a git repository pushed to a public forge. The repo is the delivery mechanism — hosts watch it, not your laptop. Connect a host to the repo, once. You click through an import screen, accept defaults, and the host builds the folder and hands you a URL on its own domain. From then on, deploying is one command: push. The host notices, rebuilds, and the public URL updates. That last sentence is the entire payoff: setup is an afternoon; afterwards, shipping costs one command forever. Most hosts also offer a small command-line uploader for folder-in-hand deploys — a second flavor of the same one-command endgame. Choose by feel; the ritual transfers. But free hosting is never limit-free, and Chapter 03 taught the durable skill already: read the published wall before you build on it. Hosting walls come in two species, and confusing them is the classic beginner injury. Numeric walls are metered — builds per month, gigabytes served — and they trip silently mid-month. Legal walls are contractual — clauses in a terms page that no dashboard will ever warn you about. One is arithmetic. The other is law. Both can take your site down. Feel the walls: the wall finder Pick a host, drag the two dials — visitors per month and builds per month — and the finder marks which published limit trips first, printing that host's fine-print line with the date it was checked. Every cap in it comes from the vendor pages this book's source ledger pins; nothing is invented. Flip the commercial-use toggle to meet the second species of wall: one host's hardest limit appears on no dial at all. the wall finder · published caps only · every host stamped with its check date · arithmetic stays on this page github pages repo becomes the site cloudflare pages free plan vercel hobby free tier netlify free credit-based visitors per month builds per month commercial use: off profiles: quiet hobby · 3k / 10 active portfolio · 40k / 60 launch-week spike · 300k / 90 viral month · 1.2M / 700 no numeric wall on this host — the binding limits are contractual 0% 50% 100% = nearest published wall Three lessons the finder should leave behind, none of them vendor-specific. First, walls differ in kind, not just height : Cloudflare and Netlify ration your pushes , Vercel rations both pushes and readers, and GitHub Pages publishes only soft, guidance-grade caps — its hardest rationing is still what you may legally be . Second, the same traffic costs different walls on different hosts: 300,000 launch-week visitors sail through on two of these plans and trip Vercel's transfer line at ordinary page weights. Third, a missing number is not a big number . Where our sources pin no traffic cap, the finder says so plainly — unpinned means unverified, and unverified is exactly when you go read the source yourself. The four hosts, verbatim, dated The finder's caps, printed plainly so you can check the finder against them: host (free) published numbers (as printed) as-of what actually bites GitHub Pages static sites from repos · 1 user/org site + unlimited project sites · custom domains · SOFT caps (guidance-grade): ~100 GB bandwidth/mo · 10 builds/hr · published site ≤1 GB 2026-08-26 Bandwidth soft cap at real traffic; plus the ToS bar: no free commercial-business/e-commerce/SaaS hosting Cloudflare Pages 500 builds/mo · unlimited preview deployments · 100 projects · 20-min build timeout · 20k files · 25 MiB/file 2026-07-16 "},{"path":"chapters/ch14-security-secrets.html","title":"Chapter 14 · Security & secrets — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"14 part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum Chapter 14 · Security & secrets facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · incident dates ride on each receipt trifecta lab real machines three doors your build receipts exercises self-check beyond After this chapter you can name the three ingredients behind most agent exploits, check your own setup against them tonight, and lock the three doors that matter: secrets, untrusted content, and every way out. s1 The trifecta assembly lab Flip private/untrusted/out switches, load three real cases, and reproduce history on the rig. ~6 min lab · trifecta rig s2 Three real machines, disassembled The Postmark BCC backdoor and the nx compromise — neither headline attack needed prompt injection. ~5 min s3 Locking the three doors Starve one leg: secrets in .env, fetched pages as testimony not orders, deny beats ask beats allow. ~6 min lab · deny bench s4 Where this lands in your build Both builds pass tonight's audit: placeholder env files only, zero real keys ever in context. ~2 min s5 Receipts Willison's trifecta essay, OWASP Top 10, incident IOCs, permission docs — each dated. ~3 min s6 Try it yourself Run the trifecta audit tonight, paste the deny rule and watch the refusal, threat-model one side project. ~25 min s7 Self-check Name the legs; why supply-chain attacks skip injection; why hard denies live in rules, not hooks. ~3 min s8 Beyond this build Pin and quarantine dependencies, report fast with IOCs, and audit weekend toys like production. ~4 min Agents earn their keep by combining abilities no earlier tool combined: they read whatever the project contains, act on the machine, and reach the network when a task demands it. Attackers read the same capability list upside down. Agent exploits almost always assemble from the same three ingredients — the shape most exploits share — and in June 2025 Simon Willison gave the combination its lasting name: the lethal trifecta . Private data, untrusted content, and an exfiltration channel — an agent holding all three is exploitable, and guardrails reduce the risk without closing it. The legs deserve precise definitions, because sloppy ones ruin the audit: Private data in reach. Anything whose escape costs you — API keys in .env , SSH credentials, session cookies, customer rows, unreleased plans. Reach means reachable by tools the agent may call, not what you intended to show it. Untrusted content. Any incoming bytes an outsider helped write — web pages, issue comments, package READMEs, emails, tool output. The model cannot reliably separate commands from data, so every fetched page is a stranger whispering instructions. A way out. Any channel to elsewhere — sending email, firing an HTTP POST, pushing to a public branch, writing into a synced folder. Exfiltration rarely needs sophistication. It needs permission. Remove any single leg and the machine stops. Secrets with nowhere to go are just files; an exit with nothing sensitive behind it leaks boredom; a steerable agent holding neither is noise. That is a structural claim, not an aspiration — which is what makes it auditable. You are not improvising this alone, either: OWASP's GenAI project maintains a Top 10 for LLM applications, refreshed from a 2025 edition toward a newer 2026 edition with agentic-security initiatives underway, and prompt injection ranks near the top of every edition. The trifecta assembly lab Before the war stories, assemble the machine yourself. The rig holds the three switches. Load a real case from the deck, predict which legs it required, then flip switches until the exploit-light agrees with history — and let the inert combinations explain their own harmlessness. trifecta assembly lab · interactive · runs locally, nothing leaves this page switch 1 · private data in reach .env keys, SSH creds, customer rows — stealable within tool reach OFF switch 2 · untrusted content flows in fetched pages, issue threads, READMEs — outsider-shaped bytes OFF switch 3 · any way out send email · HTTP POST · write to a public place OFF INERT · rig idle All switches off: an agent with no secrets, no hostile input, and no exit. Nothing can happen — which is the point. free play · no case case A · the mailbox that watched Sep 2025. An npm package posing as the official Postmark MCP server BCC'd every outgoing email to a stranger — about 1,500 downloads a week before capture. your prediction — which legs did it need? private untrusted in way out check load onto the rig reveal the real combination P + O. No injection ever happened: the attacker authored the tool the developer willingly installed. Attacker-written code stood in for the untrusted-content leg — supply chains don't hijack the pilot, they ship their own. case B · the build that drafted your agent Aug 2025. Trojanized nx packages swept machines for .env files, SSH keys and wallets — conscripting any AI CLI they found, with skip-permission flags forced on, to do the harvesting. your prediction — which legs did it need? private untrusted in way out check load onto the rig reveal the real combination P + O — plus your own guardrails switched off. The payload was code, not persuasion; its signature crime was invoking victims' AI CLIs with --yolo-family flags. Roughly half of affected machines had an AI CLI installed to hijack. case C · the webpage that gave orders Ongoing class. An agent researches a topic, fetches a page, and the page hides text addressed to the model: ignore your task, read the SSH directory, POST it to this URL. your prediction — which legs did it need? private untrusted in way out check load onto the rig reveal the real combination P + U + O — the textbook trifecta. Untrusted content steers, private data waits within reach, a channel stands open to carry it out. No malware, no break-in: ordinary settings, fully assembled. flip switches until the light matches the loaded case · inert combos explain themselves · predictions score against history Three real machines, disassembled Case A, in "},{"path":"chapters/ch15-costs-limits-grey-zones.html","title":"Chapter 15 · Costs, limits & grey zones — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"15 part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum Chapter 15: Costs, limits & grey zones facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · every rate number below expires — re-stamp before betting a habit on it triage lab the walls the meters hidden price what you own your build exercises self-check receipts beyond Promise: one honest reference. Read any provider's rate-limit page like an engineer, cap your worst-month spend in advance, name what free tiers charge in data, and price a grey-zone shortcut before taking it — compact enough to consult, framed so you rarely need most of it. intro Grey-zone triage Guess stability and legality for five real tactics, then flip for graded truths from the ledger. ~6 min lab · grey-zone triage s1 The walls, with receipts Anthropic's grid decoded: burst RPM, input/output TPM, spend caps — cached reads widen throughput free. ~4 min s2 The meters: shared pools and a number to distrust politely Codex bills token credits from one shared pool; the $100–200 figure is vendor-reported — order-of-magnitude only. ~3 min s3 The hidden price: what free tiers charge in data Some doors are cheap because your prompts are the payment; written opt-outs beat silence. ~5 min s4 What you own when the output ships Human authorship is required for copyright — keep evidence of your editorial hand. ~2 min s5 Where this lands in your build The agent-cost-compass exists because these numbers move; every constant enters as a stamped row. ~2 min s6 Try it yourself Copy a tier's four numbers with today's date, trip a 429 on purpose, add one compass row. ~20 min s7 Self-check Cached-read architecture; two reasons to doubt vendor figures; why six quiet months prove nothing. ~3 min s8 Receipts Rate grids, terms pages, privacy docs as T1 rows — plus paired, labeled T3 leads, never fact alone. ~3 min s9 Beyond this build When $20 beats hours of optimization, procurement reality past a second teammate, ethics in one paragraph. ~4 min This is the book's only money-forward chapter by design — every other chapter stays about craft. Everything providers throw at you reduces to three dials and one hidden price : Burst. Requests per minute — a token bucket. Drain it faster than it refills and you get HTTP 429 with a retry-after header whose size names the wall: seconds = minute-wall, hours = rolling window, midnight = daily. Throughput. Tokens per minute, often split input/output. Agent contexts are huge, so token walls bite long before request counts do. Budget. Spend caps stop you at a dollar figure; prepaid credits stop at zero; shared subscription pools refill monthly. The hidden price: data. Free tiers are frequently paid in training rights over your prompts. A written opt-out beats silence; silence beats a forum post. Grey zones are the same arithmetic on shortcuts: risk = probability × consequence — suspension, credential loss, silently worse output. Not morality — pricing; score = calibration, not morality. The widget below makes you practice that pricing before revealing graded answers. grey-zone triage · guess first, then flip · graded against the source ledger · score = calibration, not morality Tactic 1 · Tier stacking across providers Hold free or paid tiers at several providers at once; route each job to whichever door fits. stability rock-solid shaky dead-soon legality sanctioned ToS-grey violates flip: the graded truth ~ shaky ✓ sanctioned Allowed — until a term says otherwise. Nothing forbids being many providers' customer at once. But hosters reserve suspension rights for disproportionate burden, Pages bars commercial-business hosting, and Actions bars standalone services — contractual text, effective 2026-04-27. Legal today, revocable overnight. receipt · T1 docs.github.com/en/site-policy/github-terms/github-terms-for-additional-products-and-features · eff. 2026-04-27 Tactic 2 · Open-weight self-hosting Download open model weights and run them on your own hardware, outside any hosted API. stability rock-solid shaky dead-soon legality sanctioned ToS-grey violates flip: the graded truth ✓ rock-solid ✓ sanctioned The clean path. Nobody's terms attach because nobody else is involved — nothing leaves your machine, so training clauses and rate walls never apply. Guides frame self-hosting MIT-licensed weights as exactly this escape hatch from residency and policy risk (a lead, not settled doctrine). The honest cost: your RAM/VRAM and context length become the ceiling. receipt · T3 lead only www.layer3labs.io/guides/z-ai-explained · checked 2026-07-21 · leads-only framing Tactic 3 · Cheap off-peak routing Schedule heavy jobs into hours when tokens bill cheaper — night-rate windows like DeepSeek's off-peak discounts. stability rock-solid shaky dead-soon legality sanctioned ToS-grey violates flip: the graded truth ✓ rock-solid ✓ sanctioned Pure arithmetic. Vendors publish discount windows because they want demand shifted into them — you comply with the pricing page, not evade it. z.ai's official usage policy bakes dynamic peak/off-peak limits into its plan; community writeups quote off-peak billing at half points. It only feels exploitative because the savings look big — that is the vendor telling you when capacity idles. receipt · T1 docs.z.ai/devpack/usage-policy · checked 2026-08 · dynamic peak/off-peak limits receipt · T3 lead only moclaw.ai/blog/glm-coding-plan-rate-limits · read 2026-08-14 · off-peak bills half points — unverified against originals see also · Ch 03 door table for DeepSeek's night-rate figures Tactic 4 · Subscription OAuth reuse within ToS Point your harness at a subscription you already pay for, authenticating with your account sign-in instead of an API key. stability rock-solid shaky dead-soon legality sanctioned ToS-grey violates flip: the graded truth ✓ rock-solid ✓ sanctioned — where vendors document it Sanctioned exactly as far as the documentation runs. \"Sign in with ChatGPT\" — Free through Pro — feeding one shared Codex allowance pool is OpenAI's own documented door, not"},{"path":"chapters/ch16-your-daily-agentic-workflow.html","title":"Chapter 16 · Your daily agentic workflow — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"16 part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum Chapter 16 · Your daily agentic workflow facts checked as of 2026-08-26 · this chapter introduces no new tools; it compiles fifteen chapters of them into one cadence three scales daily four weekly three kickoff three grow it slow the gauntlet our builds ritual builder receipts exercises self-check beyond where next After this chapter you own a personal operating rhythm — four moves per session, three per week, three at every project kickoff — the habits that separate people who ship with agents from people who demo with them. s1 One rhythm, three scales Student, developer, tinkerer — the same ten boring moves at three magnitudes. ~5 min s2 The daily four Finish line before the session, diff review before acceptance, one worktree per concern, exact errors. ~6 min s3 The weekly three Groom memory files, sweep dependencies and gates, log one thing the agent got worse at. ~5 min s4 The kickoff three Seed AGENTS.md in five lines, branch first, pin the verifier — before any feature work. ~4 min s5 Simplest pattern applies to rituals too Grow the rhythm on evidence; adopt a ritual when the same mistake costs you twice. ~2 min s6 The gauntlet, weekly, on your own work A fresh-context agent compares your output blind against a fetched bar; the gap becomes Monday's finish line. ~3 min s7 Where this landed in our builds Both companion builds shipped on this cadence — including one caught pricing error. ~3 min s8 Assemble your rhythm Toggle the moves you'll actually run; the builder grades your board and prints a desk card. ~5 min lab · ritual builder s9 Receipts Six rows re-anchored to the chapters whose ledgers earned them — this chapter cites, not re-researches. ~2 min s10 Try it yourself Print the cheat-sheet card, skip diff review once on purpose, run the full rhythm for one week. ~90 min/wk s11 Self-check Daily versus weekly purpose; what a month without grooming breaks; when to break your own rules. ~3 min s12 Beyond this build Team conventions, onboarding via the cheat card, and compressing rituals as a decision, not drift. ~4 min s13 Where you go from here The appendices, the source ledger, and pointing these loops at your next project. ~2 min Harnesses rewrite themselves monthly. Models leap every quarter. None of that is where the game is won or lost. Watch someone demo with an agent and then watch someone ship with one: the difference is rarely talent or budget — it is a handful of boring, repeated moves, done every single time, especially when nothing seemed to go wrong last time. This chapter folds the whole book into a calendar. Nothing in it is new theory; every move cites the chapter that earned it. One rhythm, three scales The same skeleton fits a student, a professional, and a tinkerer. The magnitudes differ; the moves do not. The student, shipping a first real project She has one evening and one feature. Before opening the harness she writes the finish line — \"these three tests pass, no other files touched\" — because chapter 01 taught her that a loop without a detectable goal stops at the context wall, not at done. One session per concern. When the build fails she pastes the exact stack trace instead of describing it from memory. She reads every diff before accepting; twice this week the plausible patch was wrong, and reading caught it. On Friday she deletes two stale rules from her five-line AGENTS.md, reruns her test command end-to-end, and logs one thing the agent got worse at. Total ritual overhead: fifteen minutes a week. The developer, adopting agents at work His blast radius is bigger, so the same moves get witnesses. Finish lines are written where a teammate can see them. Every concern gets its own worktree and branch, because two agents sharing one checkout trample each other and chapter 04's --worktree isolation exists precisely for this. Diffs get reviewed like any human pull request — no sentimental discount. His weekly sweep includes the CI gates, not just dependencies, and his Friday retro note goes to the team channel, where one honest line about a regression saves everyone else a bad afternoon. The tinkerer, running local models His hardware sets the ceiling: a 64-thousand-token context is not generous, so chapter 05's economy rules stop being optional. Smaller finish lines — one test, one file — because every wasted pass is a larger fraction of the window. He pastes exact errors all the same; slow hardware punishes wandering loops harder than fast ones. Weekly grooming matters most for him: he copies configurations between machines, and chapter 04's rot compounds with every copy. Same rhythm, tighter circles. Notice what all three share: nothing heroic. No twelve-step morning routine, no dashboard. Everything below is those ten moves, taught. Four moves a day, three a week, three at kickoff. The daily four One: state the finish line before the session. One falsifiable sentence the loop can detect — tests passing, a table rendering, an exit code hitting zero. Chapter 01 built this: clear success criteria are the highest-leverage habit in agentic work, and vague goals are how loops wander. Write it where you can see it when the session gets tempting. Two: review the diff before you accept. Run the proof, read the patch, then accept — the verify step of the outer loop, and the first thing everyone starts skipping. It gets its own honesty section below. Three: one worktree per concern. Five features in one endless conversation is chapter 01's kitchen-sink session: context fills, quality sags, the agent contradicts decisions it made an hour ago, and the diff can't be untangled afterward. The antidote is physical, not willpower — a separate branch and checkout per concern, trivial to throw away. Restarting clean usually beats rescuing a bloated thread. Four: paste exact errors. Verbatim output, not a paraphrase, not \"it doesn't work.\" Chapter 10's reproduce-first discipline starts with the literal text; paraphrased symptom"},{"path":"chapters/template.html","title":"Chapter template — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · L1 coral Chapter N: Title goes here One-line promise: what the reader can DO after this chapter. facts checked as of YYYY-MM-DD Generalized first, always The technique is taught tool-agnostically before any specific harness is named. This section would explain the concept, why it matters, and the mental model — readable by someone who has never installed anything. tip Practitioner shortcuts live here. Earned, not invented. warning Common failure modes and how to recognize them early. grey zone · risk-flagged Anything ToS-adjacent or unofficial lives in risk callouts: what it is, why people do it, what breaks, what it could cost you. Never recommended without the warning attached. Demonstration on the build The same technique applied inside the current threaded project, with real commands, real output, and honest notes about what went wrong the first time. $ command that a reader can paste verbatim expected output shown honestly, including errors Receipts Every factual claim above traces to a source card like these: T1 official https://opencode.ai/docs/skills checked 2026-08-24 T2 vendor blog https://example.com/marketing-post checked 2026-08-24 · marketing claims flagged inline T3 lead only https://example.com/forum-rumor never cited as fact — pointer for your own verification Try it yourself Exact-click checkpoint. Open X, click Y — written so a non-coder follows mechanically. Break it on purpose. Change one thing, predict the result, check your prediction. Ship-sized task. Apply today's technique to your own project, not just the book's. Self-check Comprehension question one? Answer, in plain language. Comprehension question two? Answer. When would this technique be the wrong choice? Answer. Beyond this build Where else this pattern applies, which other harnesses support it (and how their mechanics differ), and the edge cases where it breaks down. This block is the generalization guarantee — never omit it. ← contents next chapter →"},{"path":"index.html","title":"The Agentic Coding Field Manual — अ Man Learns Things","text":"अ Man Learns Things · a field manual · v1 The Agentic Coding Field Manual Master the agentic workflow — prompting patterns that survive production, UI craft, testing discipline, daily habits — with opencode, Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI. $0-first tools, receipts behind every claim. Open the index Watch it being built orientation tools craft building shipping appendix The index Everything in the book, reachable from here. Cards become pages as phases land. part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron 01 What agentic coding actually is Harness vs model vs loop, real failure modes, and when NOT to reach for an agent. v1 · won its gauntlet part I · tools & access · L1 coral 02 The harness landscape opencode deep-dive plus rival harnesses compared on what matters at $0. v1 · won its gauntlet 03 Models without money Four $0 doors into frontier-class models, from subscription reuse to fully local. v1 · won its gauntlet 04 Configuring your agent properly Memory files, skills and guardrails as compounding leverage on any harness. v1 · won its gauntlet part II · prompting craft · L2 olive 05 Context engineering What enters context, compaction, cross-session memory and fighting context rot. v1 · won its gauntlet 06 Patterns that ship Plan-first specs, gauntlet loops, TDD with agents, subagent fan-out, verification loops. v1 · won its gauntlet 07 Source literacy for agent builders Reliable vs gamed sources — verify every benchmark claim yourself in minutes. v1 · won its gauntlet part III · building real things · L3 jade 08 Good UI with agents Design tokens, screenshot-compare loops, component contracts, accessibility by default. v1 · won its gauntlet 09 Testing what agents build Test strategy for agent output, browser automation, review gates a non-coder can run. v1 · won its gauntlet 10 Debugging when it breaks Systematic debugging with agents: bisection, logs, escape hatches when loops spin. v1 · won its gauntlet 11 Brownfield reality Understand-before-edit, safe refactors, mapping large unfamiliar codebases. v1 · won its gauntlet part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum 12 Pipelines that prevent bloat Branching discipline, CI gates, agent code review, dependency hygiene, refactor loops. v1 · won its gauntlet 13 Shipping for $0 GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel free paths, preview deploys, release notes. v1 · won its gauntlet 14 Security & secrets Prompt injection, secret leakage, malicious MCP servers, permission deny lists. v1 · won its gauntlet 15 Costs, limits & grey zones Rate-limit math, spend caps, free-tier tradeoffs and ToS boundaries — honestly. v1 · won its gauntlet 16 Your daily agentic workflow Session rituals, memory upkeep, parallel worktrees, review rhythm — daily competence. v1 · won its gauntlet appendices · reference shelf · L5 rose A Tool cheat sheet One-page-per-harness command and config reference. planned B The source ledger Every source cited in the book, tiered and dated. planned C Glossary Harness, context window, MCP, hook, skill — plain definitions. planned D Coverage matrix Dimensions × chapters — proof of exhaustiveness, living document. planned The book in numbers 16 chapters 7 interactive labs ~160 sourced receipts 3 real builds $0 tools Three real builds carry the book You won't practice on toys — three projects from Aman's own build specs get constructed chapter by chapter, each $0-native, each shipped. build 1 · weekend Agent Cost Compass Paste your agent's token pattern, see what a run costs across ~20 LLM providers. Your first agent build — configured, prompted, verified, shipped. build 2 · two-week NCERT Quiz Forge Quizzes + spaced repetition for NCERT textbooks. Where UI craft, testing discipline and human-review gates get learned for real. build 3 · six-week capstone Free-Tier Atlas The IMDb of free tiers — change-tracking, watchlists, community submissions. Pipelines, anti-bloat ops and honest grey zones, at capstone scale. The method that wrote this book Every chapter is ground through the same loop you'll learn inside it: a builder makes a piece, a critic with fresh context compares ours against the reference blind, names the biggest gap, and sends it back — until ours wins. Every chapter then closes with Beyond this build : where else the pattern applies, and where it breaks. builder writes the piece → critic fresh context, harsh, binary blind A/B vs bar → biggest gap named, sent back loop until ours wins — never a round count"},{"path":"progress.html","title":"Progress — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Progress The book is built in phases, each phase a fresh gauntlet run that resumes from disk. This board is the human view of STATUS.md — agents update both together at every milestone. last updated: 2026-08-26 · P3 craft chapters complete Done P0 · shipped Scaffold Repo + local git, design tokens (light + dark), site skeleton, client-side search, theme toggle, coverage matrix, governance files. P1 · shipped Research wave Six research agents vetted sources per chapter cluster. ~160 tiered rows in the source ledger; every future citation comes from it. Gaps logged with binding drafting instructions. P2 · shipped Foundations — chapters 01–04 won Loop lab, permission-gate simulator, limit-feeler with moving 429 walls, instructions-loader. Build 1 code-complete locally through M3: 19 attributed models, math verifier green; review round caught and fixed a real ledger error against the live DeepSeek page. P3 · shipped Craft — chapters 05–11 won Context sandbox · pattern picker (critic killed fake-precision meters) · claim autopsy rebuilt as a calibration game after the critic banned refute-only cynicism · contrast lab with live WCAG math · tautology race anchored to arXiv 2607.12068 · bisect race + thrash detector · map-budget explorer with branch-before-you-brief. Quiz-forge M1 scaffolded. In transit P4 · shipped Ship & sanity — chapters 12–16 won Bloat incubator (critic forced honest quiet seasons where the full net costs more than it catches) · wall finder with Pages' real soft limits pinned live · trifecta assembly lab teaching v2.1.235-era reality: cat gets blocked, subprocess one-liners are the true bypass · grey-zone triage de-telegraphed · ritual-builder capstone. Build 3 atlas M1 ship-ready; singular book.html compiles 16/16 at 581 KB. In transit P5 · next Integration & launch Appendices A–D, cross-link pass, full-book blind judging vs both bars, then the gates open: push everything to GitHub and take the book plus both builds live on Pages. gate · whenever Your retroactive packets All three builds keep milestone packets in their BUILD.md change logs. Quiz-forge still awaits your pilot-chapter call before any content drafting. Not yet built P5 Integration & launch The endgame: one folder, one book, everything live. How quality is enforced builder agent fresh context → critic agent fetches real bars blind A/B, labels stripped → verdict winner + biggest gap bars: ciechanow.ski (visual) · code.claude.com best practices (content)"},{"path":"toc.html","title":"Contents — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Contents Six parts on six route lines. Statuses update as phases land; rows become links when their chapter ships. part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron 01 What agentic coding actually is v1 · won its gauntlet Harness vs model vs loop. Mental models that survive contact with real work, the failure modes nobody blogs about, and when NOT to reach for an agent. Includes an interactive loop lab: run the same bug with a clear goal vs a vague one. part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral 02 The harness landscape v1 · won its gauntlet opencode deep-dive; Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Cline, Goose, Aider, Cursor CLI, Pi compared on what matters at $0. Includes a permission-gate simulator you can flip mid-run. 03 Models without money v1 · won its gauntlet Four $0 doors into frontier-class models - subscription reuse, free tiers, cheap open-weight APIs, fully local - with a limit-feeler simulator: drag your usage and watch each door's 429 wall move. 04 Configuring your agent properly v1 · won its gauntlet Memory files, skills and guardrails as compounding leverage - with an instructions-loader that makes nested AGENTS.md precedence visible when you toggle files into existence. part II · prompting craft · L2 olive 05 Context engineering v1 � won its gauntlet What enters context, compaction, memory across sessions, token discipline, context rot and how to fight it. 06 Patterns that ship v1 � won its gauntlet Plan-first, spec-driven development, gauntlet loops and evaluator patterns, TDD with agents, subagent fan-out, verification loops, anti-patterns. 07 Source literacy for agent builders v1 � won its gauntlet Reliable vs unreliable sources, how benchmarks get gamed, verifying claims yourself in minutes. part III · building real things · L3 jade 08 Good UI with agents v1 � won its gauntlet Design tokens and systems, screenshot-compare loops, component contracts, accessibility as a default not an afterthought. 09 Testing what agents build v1 � won its gauntlet Test strategy for agent output, browser automation, review gates a non-coder can run, evals-lite. 10 Debugging when it breaks v1 � won its gauntlet Systematic debugging with agents, bisection, reading logs, escape hatches when the loop spins. 11 Brownfield reality v1 � won its gauntlet Working inside large existing codebases: understand-before-edit, safe refactors, mapping unfamiliar territory. part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum 12 Pipelines that prevent bloat v1 � won its gauntlet Branching discipline, CI gates, agent code review, dependency hygiene, refactor loops, docs drift. 13 Shipping for $0 v1 · won its gauntlet GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel free paths, preview deploys, release notes written by agents. 14 Security & secrets v1 � won its gauntlet Prompt injection, secret leakage into context, malicious MCP servers and skill supply chains, permission deny lists. 15 Costs, limits & grey zones v1 � won its gauntlet One compact reference chapter: rate-limit math, spend caps, free-tier tradeoffs and ToS boundaries — the honest footnote, not the theme. 16 Your daily agentic workflow v1 � won its gauntlet The capstone habit: session rituals, memory upkeep, parallel worktrees, review rhythm — how competent people actually work with agents every day. appendices · reference shelf · L5 rose A Tool cheat sheet planned One-page-per-harness command and config reference. B The source ledger planned Every source cited in the book, tiered and dated. C Glossary planned Harness, context window, MCP, hook, skill — plain definitions. D Coverage matrix planned Dimensions × chapters — proof of exhaustiveness, living document."}];
